    id: 'case-54',
    pharmacologySubject: 'General Pharmacology',
    unit: 'Therapeutic Drug Monitoring',
    learningObjectives: ['Interpret lithium levels', 'Manage lithium toxicity'],
    specialty: 'Psychiatric Disorders',
    disease: 'Therapeutic Drug Monitoring',
    title: 'Lithium Toxicity in a Patient with Bipolar Disorder',
    difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    demographics: '42-year-old female, Luhya teacher from Kakamega',
    chiefComplaint: 'Severe tremor, slurred speech, vomiting, and confusion for 2 days.',
    hpi: 'Ms. Munyala has bipolar I disorder on Lithium 300mg TID. She developed a febrile illness with gastroenteritis 4 days ago and stopped drinking fluids. She continued her lithium. Now she presents with coarse tremor, ataxia, dysarthria, vomiting, and confusion.',
    pmh: 'Bipolar I disorder (diagnosed 8 years, stable on lithium), Hypothyroidism (on levothyroxine).',
    medHx: 'Lithium Carbonate 300mg TID (3 x 300mg = 900mg/day), Levothyroxine 100mcg daily, Olanzapine 10mg at bedtime.',
    allergies: 'NKDA',
    pe: 'General: Confused, drowsy, dehydrated. Neuro: Coarse bilateral tremor, ataxia, nystagmus, hyperreflexia, GCS 13/15. CV: Tachycardic, JVP decreased. Skin: Reduced skin turgor, dry mucous membranes.',
    vitals: 'HR: 102 bpm, BP: 100/65 mmHg, RR: 16 breaths/min, Temp: 37.0C, SpO2: 97%',
    labs: 'Serum Lithium Level: 2.8 mEq/L (therapeutic 0.6-1.2, toxic >1.5). Na+: 132 mEq/L (dehydration). Creatinine: 1.6 mg/dL (baseline 0.9). eGFR: 38 mL/min. TSH: 3.5 mIU/mL (on levothyroxine). ECG: Normal sinus rhythm, QTc 450 ms.',
    diagnosis: 'Lithium Toxicity (Moderate-Severe) Precipitated by Dehydration and Gastroenteritis.',
    ddx: ['Lithium toxicity', 'Neurologic malignant syndrome', 'Cerebrovascular accident', 'CNS infection', 'Hypoglycemia'],
    goals: 'Correct dehydration, enhance lithium elimination, prevent irreversible neurologic damage.',
    pharm: '1. HOLD Lithium immediately. 2. Aggressive IV Normal Saline rehydration (1L bolus, then 200 mL/hr). 3. Correct hyponatremia (Na+ >135 mEq/L enhances lithium excretion). 4. Check levels every 6-12 hours. 5. If level >3.0 or severe symptoms (seizures, coma): hemodialysis. 6. Restart lithium at reduced dose once stable (300mg BID or 450mg daily depending on renal function).',
    nonPharm: 'Continuous monitoring. Strict I/O. Prevent falls due to ataxia.',
    carePlan: 'Admit to medical ward. IV fluids. Hold lithium. Monitor lithium levels and renal function. Psychiatry consult.',
    dtps: '1. Drug toxicity: Lithium accumulation due to dehydration and prerenal AKI. 2. Need for TDM and patient education on sick-day management.',
    monitoring: 'Lithium level every 6-12 hours, Na+, K+, creatinine daily, neuro status (GCS, tremor), ECG.',
    counselling: 'Lithium is a salt - it follows sodium. When dehydrated, the kidneys reabsorb sodium AND lithium, causing toxic levels. Sick-day rules: If you have vomiting/diarrhea/fever, HOLD lithium and drink fluids. Seek medical help. Get lithium levels checked every 3-6 months.',
    followUp: 'Psychiatry in 1 week for lithium re-initiation and mood assessment. Recheck lithium level 1 week after restarting.',
    pearls: 'Lithium narrow therapeutic index (0.6-1.2 mEq/L). Levels >1.5 = mild toxicity, >2.5 = moderate toxicity, >3.5 = severe/nephrotoxicity. Lithium is not protein-bound, not metabolized, and 100% renally cleared. Hemodialysis is the definitive treatment for severe toxicity.',
